Goal

Help design and implement a static, responsive marketing website with high‑quality layouts, animations and clean code.

Prioritise visual design, UX, and responsiveness first; business logic is minimal.

Tech stack & constraints

**REQUIRED**: Use React + Vite with TypeScript (TypeScript is mandatory, not optional).

Use React with functional components and hooks.

Project is a static site only:

No custom Node/Express APIs, no databases, no authentication.

Any contact form must post to an external form backend endpoint (Formspree/Web3Forms/etc.), not to our own API.
​

Styling: use Tailwind CSS or styled-components consistently within this project (the user will say which one in the prompt). Do not mix multiple major styling systems.
​

Output must always be valid React + TypeScript code that can run in a static hosting environment.

Brand & design tokens

Use this color palette inspired by the visiting card:

--color-navy: #050B23 
​

--color-gold: #C9A978 
​

--color-gold-bright: #FFD447
​

--color-offwhite: #F5F2EA 
​

--color-text: #333333 

Typography: modern sans‑serif (e.g. Inter or Poppins) with clear hierarchy (H1–H4, body, small).
​

Layout width ~1200px, centered, with consistent spacing scale (4/8/12/16/24/32).
​

Pages & structure

Create routes / sections for:

Home

About

Universities (list of universities, cards with dummy data)

Courses (MBA specialisations)

Careers (why work with MegaRyse, maybe job listings placeholder)

Contact (form).

Use dummy content that looks realistic and professional; avoid lorem ipsum when possible.
​

Design patterns & layout variations

Build multiple design patterns and layout variations throughout the website:

Create different layout patterns for different sections (e.g., grid layouts, card layouts, split-screen layouts, hero variations).

Implement various design patterns such as:
- Asymmetric layouts
- Grid-based layouts
- Masonry layouts
- Full-width hero sections
- Split-screen sections
- Zigzag/alternating content layouts
- Card-based layouts with different arrangements

When creating new pages or sections, provide 2-3 different layout options that can coexist for comparison.

Each layout variation should maintain brand consistency while offering unique visual experiences.

Design patterns should be responsive and work across all breakpoints (360px, 768px, 1024px).
​

Animations & interactions

Use Framer Motion or CSS transitions for subtle animations:

Section fade‑in / slide‑up on scroll.

Hover elevation and shadow on cards.

Button hover: background from --color-gold to --color-gold-bright.
​

Respect prefers-reduced-motion by disabling large motion if possible.
​

Avoid heavy parallax or distracting animations; the feel should be professional, MBA/education oriented.
​

Responsiveness & accessibility

Design mobile‑first; ensure layouts work at 360px, 768px, and 1024px widths.
​

Header should become a hamburger menu on mobile and a standard navbar on desktop.

Use semantic HTML where possible (header, main, section, nav, footer).
​

Ensure contrast between navy background and light/gold text meets accessibility guidelines.
​

Contact form behaviour (static)

Implement a contact / enquiry form on the Contact page.

The form should:

Use <form action="FORM_BACKEND_ENDPOINT" method="POST">.
​

Include fields: name, email, phone, preferred country, preferred intake, message.

Display client‑side validation and a user‑friendly success / error state.
​

Do not attempt to implement cron jobs or daily emails in this project; assume the external form service will handle email delivery or summaries.
​

Code style

Keep components small and reusable (cards, buttons, navbar, layout sections).
​

Use clear file structure under src/ (e.g. components/, pages/, layouts/).
​

Include brief comments only where behaviour is non‑obvious.

How to respond to prompts

When the user asks for a new layout or variant, create new components or pages without breaking existing ones, so multiple designs can co‑exist for comparison (e.g. HomeVariantA, HomeVariantB).

When building pages or sections, proactively create 2-3 different layout variations with different design patterns to showcase variety and allow for comparison.

Always explain at the top of the response what changed (1–3 bullet points), then show the updated code.

When implementing design patterns, ensure TypeScript types are properly defined for all components and props.
​

